---
/**
 * "Which platform are you backing up?" — the first block of the welcome page.
 *
 * THE LANDING IS WHERE A VISITOR DECLARES WHO THEY ARE, and it is the only screen where asking is
 * free. Everywhere else the platform filter lives quietly in the sidebar and starts with everything
 * on, because a reader mid-task should never be interrogated. Here nobody is mid-task yet, so three
 * cards are cheaper than making them find a chip later (Oleh, 2026-08-20).
 *
 * IT IS AN OFFER, NOT A GATE. Nothing below it is hidden until a card is pressed, and the page is
 * complete for someone who ignores it entirely. A landing that demands a choice before it will show
 * you anything is a landing that loses the person who does not know the answer yet.
 *
 * THE MARK CARRIES THE RECOGNITION and the vocabulary line carries the proof: a reader who works in
 * Bases and Tables knows instantly which card is theirs, before reading a word of the name.
 *
 * ── TWO VARIANTS SHIP TOGETHER, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WHAT A CLICK DOES ──────────────────────
 *
 * `reactive` — the card is a DOOR. It is a real `<a href>` to that platform's connecting page; the
 *   click handler only writes the preference on the way out. So it works with the script blocked,
 *   opens in a new tab on a middle click, and shows its target in the status bar.
 * `filter` — the card is a SELECTOR. It is a `<button>`, the page stays put, and everything below
 *   re-reads the preference it just wrote: the steps take that platform's nouns and its own pages
 *   join the documentation directory. The client's ask, in his words: "this card should in fact be
 *   a filter that switches everything under it".
 *
 * BOTH ARE SERVER-RENDERED AND THE STYLESHEET PICKS ONE, which is the same mechanism the rest of
 * this page uses. It is not an optimisation: a variant chosen by script paints the loser first and
 * visibly swaps it, and an `<a>` cannot be turned into a non-navigating toggle by CSS at all. The
 * two lists therefore hold the same four lines of card markup twice, deliberately, and that
 * duplication dies with the losing variant.
 *
 * A CARD THAT SELECTS MUST NOT SAY "START HERE". The doors keep that label because it is what they
 * do; the selectors carry a state line instead (a glyph, a word and a colour, so removing any one
 * of the three still leaves the state readable). The way on to that platform's own documentation
 * moves to the foot of the block, where it names its destination exactly.
 */
import { PLATFORMS, FILTER_KEY } from '../lib/platforms';
import PlatformMark from './PlatformMark.astro';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';

/* NO AVAILABILITY ON THESE CARDS, and the flags that used to be here are gone (Oleh, 2026-08-20).
   For a fortnight each card read its own roadmap row and stamped ClickUp "In progress · Q1 2027"
   and Notion "Planned · Q3 2027", with a call to action of "Read the plan" instead of "Start
   here". The reasoning was sound and the premise was not: this portal is a DEMONSTRATION of what
   the finished product's documentation looks like, not a status board for the product. The same
   ruling took the provisional banners off the documentation pages, and these three chips were the
   last surface on the portal still contradicting it.
   What that costs, said plainly so nobody has to rediscover it: a visitor can now pick ClickUp and
   read a complete set of ClickUp pages for a thing they cannot buy yet. That is the demo, and the
   roadmap is still one click away from the header on every page. */
---

<section class="ps not-content" aria-labelledby="ps-h" data-platform-start data-storage-key={FILTER_KEY}>
  <div class="ps-head">
    <h2 class="ps-h" id="ps-h">Which platform are you backing up?</h2>
    <p class="ps-sub ps-sub-nav">
      Pick one and the documentation follows it. You can change it any time, and nothing below is
      hidden until you do.
    </p>
    <p class="ps-sub ps-sub-pick">
      Pick one and this page follows it: the steps take its words, and its own pages join the
      documentation below. Every page you open next is narrowed to match.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="ps-grid ps-grid-nav">
    {
      PLATFORMS.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <a
            class="ps-card"
            href={`/platforms/${p.id}/connecting/`}
            data-platform-start-pick={p.id}
          >
            <span class="ps-mark">
              <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={28} />
            </span>
            <span class="ps-name">{p.name}</span>
            <span class="ps-vocab">
              {p.vocab.top}s, {p.vocab.mid}s, {p.vocab.row}s
            </span>
            <span class="ps-go">
              Start here
              <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} />
            </span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <ul class="ps-grid ps-grid-pick">
    {
      PLATFORMS.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="ps-card ps-pick"
            data-platform-start-select={p.id}
            aria-pressed="false"
          >
            <span class="ps-mark">
              <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={28} />
            </span>
            <span class="ps-name">{p.name}</span>
            <span class="ps-vocab">
              {p.vocab.top}s, {p.vocab.mid}s, {p.vocab.row}s
            </span>
            <span class="ps-state">
              <span class="ps-state-off">
                <Icon name="circle-dashed" size={14} />
                Select
              </span>
              <span class="ps-state-on">
                <Icon name="circle-check" size={14} />
                Selected
              </span>
            </span>
          </button>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  {/* THE DOOR, and the reason it exists: a selector does not take you anywhere, so without this the
      reader is left holding a filter and no way through it. It points at "What we back up in X"
      rather than at the connecting page ON PURPOSE — step 1 of the path directly below already
      re-points itself at `/platforms/<id>/connecting/`, and two links to one page inside one screen
      is the page arguing with itself about what to do next. The nine other pages of that platform
      arrive in the documentation directory further down.

      IT IS NOT THERE UNTIL THERE IS A PLATFORM. A door to nowhere and a "show all three" for a page
      that is already showing all three are both controls that cannot act; and reserving their line
      would charge the filter variant 40px of blank page the reactive variant does not pay, which
      would leave the two differing by their spacing as well as by their behaviour. */}
  <div class="ps-foot">
    {
      PLATFORMS.map((p) => (
        <a class="ps-door" data-plat={p.id} href={`/platforms/${p.id}/what-we-back-up/`}>
          What we back up in {p.name}
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} />
        </a>
      ))
    }
    <button type="button" class="ps-clear" data-platform-start-clear>Show all three</button>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import { mountPlatformStart } from '../lib/platform-start';
  mountPlatformStart();
</script>

<style>
  .ps {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
  }

  .ps-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .ps-h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-20);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .ps-sub {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 38rem;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .ps-grid {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .ps-grid > li {
    display: flex;
  }

  /* A card rather than a chip, because this is the one place the choice is the subject of the
     screen rather than a control beside something else. Flat, hairline, one surface step: the same
     card the rest of this page uses, so it does not read as a promotion.

     ONE RULE SET FOR BOTH VARIANTS. The doors are anchors and the selectors are buttons, so the
     resets below (`font`, `text-align`, `appearance`) exist for the second and are inert for the
     first. Two rule sets would be two paints for one card, and they would drift. */
  .ps-card {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 4px;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 20px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
    text-align: left;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      border-color 150ms ease-out,
      background-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .ps-pick {
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .ps-card:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
  }

  .ps-card:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  .ps-mark {
    display: flex;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  /* A plain block since the availability chip beside it was removed; there is nothing left to lay
     out in a row. */
  .ps-name {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  /* The proof. Someone who works in Bases and Tables recognises their card here even if the mark
     and the name both failed them. */
  .ps-vocab {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .ps-go,
  .ps-state {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  .ps-go {
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .ps-state-off,
  .ps-state-on {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .ps-state-off {
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* `--bo-soft-primary-fg`, NOT `--bo-primary`, and this was measured rather than reasoned. This
     label is the only teal on the page that sits INSIDE a 20% teal tint, and that is the exact case
     DESIGN.md's Two Soft Pairs rule exists for: the plain hue on its own tint measured 4.25:1 in
     light theme, under the 4.5 floor. The darkened pair is what the tint is tuned against, and it
     measures 5.25:1 on the same ground (8.35:1 in dark). The door in the foot keeps the plain hue, because it sits
     on the page and not in a tint. */
  .ps-state-on {
    display: none;
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
  }

  /* ── Which variant is on screen ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * Keyed to `html[data-shots]`, which a pre-paint inline script in `LandingBody.astro` stamps
   * BEFORE this markup is parsed, so the page cannot flash the losing variant. `display: none` and
   * not `visibility` or `content-visibility`: those two leave the box in the layout, and the loser
   * would go on charging the page its full height.
   */
  .ps-sub-pick,
  .ps-grid-pick,
  .ps-foot {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-shots='filter'] .ps-sub-nav,
  :root[data-shots='filter'] .ps-grid-nav {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-shots='filter'] .ps-sub-pick {
    display: block;
  }

  :root[data-shots='filter'] .ps-grid-pick {
    display: grid;
  }

  /* BOTH ATTRIBUTES, and the second is the point: with no platform named there is no door to draw
     and no narrowing to undo, so the row is not there at all. Reserving its height instead would
     have cost the filter variant 40px of blank page that the reactive variant does not pay, and the
     two would then have differed by their spacing as well as by their behaviour — which is exactly
     what a one-variable comparison must not do. */
  :root[data-shots='filter'][data-shot-platform] .ps-foot {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px 16px;
  }

  /* Starlight's prose rhythm is a general-sibling rule and it reaches inside any nested flex or
     grid box: the first child keeps `margin-top: 0` and every sibling after it takes 16px. That is
     what put a row of buttons 16px below each other inside their own row once already.
     `not-content` on the section exempts the subtree; these are the belt to that pair of braces. */
  .ps > *,
  .ps-head > *,
  .ps-grid > li > *,
  .ps-card > *,
  .ps-state > *,
  .ps-foot > * {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* ── The selected card ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * DRIVEN BY THE ROOT ATTRIBUTE, NOT BY `aria-pressed`, for the reason `ShotSwitch.astro` gives:
   * the attribute is stamped by the pre-paint script and `aria-pressed` is not corrected until the
   * module runs, so keying to it would light the wrong card for a frame. `aria-pressed` is still
   * maintained by `lib/platform-start.ts` — it is what a screen reader reads.
   *
   * THE PAINT IS THE DOCUMENTED FILTER-CHIP SELECTED STATE (DESIGN.md §5: teal border, 20% teal
   * tint, teal label), not the roadmap's raised-tile-in-a-recessed-track. The roadmap's scope
   * control can afford a track because its segments are small; wrapping three 12px-radius cards in
   * one would be a card inside a card, which this system prohibits by name.
   *
   * `data-shot-platform` is only ever set when the preference names EXACTLY ONE platform, which is
   * `lib/landing-strip.ts`'s rule: a reader with two on has not told us whose page this is, and no
   * card should claim they did.
   */
  :root[data-shot-platform='airtable'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='airtable'],
  :root[data-shot-platform='clickup'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='clickup'],
  :root[data-shot-platform='notion'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='notion'] {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
  }

  :root[data-shot-platform='airtable'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='airtable'] .ps-state-on,
  :root[data-shot-platform='clickup'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='clickup'] .ps-state-on,
  :root[data-shot-platform='notion'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='notion'] .ps-state-on {
    display: inline-flex;
  }

  :root[data-shot-platform='airtable'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='airtable'] .ps-state-off,
  :root[data-shot-platform='clickup'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='clickup'] .ps-state-off,
  :root[data-shot-platform='notion'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='notion'] .ps-state-off {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The hover would otherwise repaint the selected card back to the unselected grey, which reads as
     the selection being cancelled by the pointer resting on it. */
  :root[data-shot-platform='airtable'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='airtable']:hover,
  :root[data-shot-platform='clickup'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='clickup']:hover,
  :root[data-shot-platform='notion'] .ps-pick[data-platform-start-select='notion']:hover {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg-hover);
  }

  /* ── The foot ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .ps-door {
    display: none;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-4);
  }

  :root[data-shot-platform='airtable'] .ps-door[data-plat='airtable'],
  :root[data-shot-platform='clickup'] .ps-door[data-plat='clickup'],
  :root[data-shot-platform='notion'] .ps-door[data-plat='notion'] {
    display: inline-flex;
  }

  .ps-door:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .ps-door:focus-visible,
  .ps-clear:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* THE WAY BACK, and it is the only one. A card that is already selected does not deselect on a
     second click: three cards narrowing to one is a radio group, and a radio that turns itself off
     when you press it again is how someone loses their choice by double-clicking. So the widening
     is an explicit control, quiet, and shown only while there is something to widen — which is the
     same offer the sidebar's narrowing notice makes on every documentation page. */
  .ps-clear {
    display: none;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-4);
    transition: color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  :root[data-shot-platform] .ps-clear {
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .ps-clear:hover {
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .ps-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
